global warming would be a whole lot  worse if it wasn't for the world's  plants sucking up carbon dioxide  since 1960 they've absorbed around a  quarter of all  fossil fuel emissions and the amazon has  been a big part of that  it contains an estimated billion  tons of carbon but new research shows  the amazon basin  is in trouble in the west it is  absorbing slightly more carbon than is  being released from dead rotting trees  and human impact but in the south and  the east  which accounts for more than a quarter  of the amazon area has become a net  source of carbon and scientists fear  it's at a tipping point where great  swathes  could be lost in a self-perpetuating  cycle of decline  longer talking about some dystopian  future this is stuff we can see on the  ground these changes are happening here  and now  and these are in a sense the beginning  it's a warning of what is still to come  because we know we are moving towards  that inexorable  situation whereby the forest will slowly  transform into a more grassland savannah  type type ecosystem  and as a result we will start pushing  more and more carbon into the atmosphere  so what's the cause well the amazon gets  more than two  meters of rain a year on average between  a quarter and a third of that results  from moisture that's released by trees  but the amazon's climate is changing  particularly in the east  now over the last 40 years rainfall at  times of the year has decreased  by up to a third and temperatures have  increased  by up to two and a half degrees the  scientists say there is a clear link  with deforestation  in the east around a quarter of the  amazon has been cleared  mostly for agriculture more than twice  as much  as the west the fires used to clear the  forest release huge amounts of carbon  but the absence of trees also dries out  the surrounding forest  raising the risk of more fires as well  as stunting its growth  and reducing carbon uptake the  conservation charity wwf says  it's bleak but not too late to stop a  runaway decline  if brazil stops further deforestation  and switches  to more sustainable use of agricultural  land
 

